---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { SITE } from '../consts';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Work — how these systems were built"
  description="Four applications built and launched solo with AI agents as the build team, and the engineering decisions behind them — measured rather than assumed."
>
  <div class="prose">
    <h1>Work</h1>

    <p class="lead">
      Four applications, built and launched solo, with AI agents as the build team. What
      follows is <strong>how they were built</strong>, because that is the part that
      transfers. None of this is client work — all of it is mine, shipped and running.
    </p>

    <!-- Screenshot slot: an <img class="project-shot" …> as the first child of .project,
         wrapped in a.shot-link to the live app (the title links there too).
         16:9, full card width. See COOKBOOK §4. -->
    <article class="project" id="envoituresimone">
      <a class="shot-link" href="https://simone.leonardtschora.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true">
        <img
          class="project-shot"
          src="/images/work-envoituresimone.png"
          alt="The En voiture Simone map: every fuel station in France as a dot coloured from green (cheap) to red (dear), beside a list of the ten cheapest."
          width="1600"
          height="900"
          loading="lazy"
        />
      </a>
      <h2><a href="https://simone.leonardtschora.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">En voiture Simone — every fuel price in France, on one map</a></h2>
      <p class="what">A public map of today's fuel prices at every station in France, refreshed every hour from the official feed. Next, the cheapest refill near you, counting the fuel and wear spent driving there.</p>
      <p class="meta">Built solo on 3 November 2026, live the same day. Deployed on Railway. Open source.</p>
      <p class="meta">Stack: FastAPI · Leaflet and OpenStreetMap · pandas and Parquet · OpenRouteService routing · Géoplateforme geocoding · Docker.</p>

      <ul class="stats">
        <li class="stat"><span class="stat-value">1 day</span><span class="stat-label">kick-off to live</span></li>
        <li class="stat"><span class="stat-value">~9,800</span><span class="stat-label">stations, <a href="https://data.economie.gouv.fr/explore/dataset/prix-des-carburants-en-france-flux-instantane-v2/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">official feed</a></span></li>
        <li class="stat"><span class="stat-value">1 h</span><span class="stat-label">price refresh</span></li>
      </ul>

      <h3>The decisions worth naming</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>
          About one price in ten in the official feed had not been updated for more than a
          week. One Paris price dated from August 2025, and it came out as the cheapest.
          Prices older than seven days are now left out, and the map says how many.
        </li>
        <li>
          One 600 KB download an hour replaces a query per visitor. The app keeps the
          snapshot and a history of every price change, so the map never waits on the
          government API.
        </li>
        <li>
          The trip comparison, motorway against toll-free, was built and then cut. A flat
          per-kilometre toll estimate was not good enough to show drivers. Real tariffs exist
          for each entry and exit pair, as open data for one operator and as PDFs for the
          others; matching them to routes is the next version.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p class="card-links">
        <a class="button" href="https://simone.leonardtschora.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Live demo</a>
        <a class="button secondary" href="https://github.com/TschorAI/envoituresimone" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source</a>
      </p>
    </article>

    <article class="project" id="chocacao">
      <a class="shot-link" href="https://chocacao.leonardtschora.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true">
        <img
          class="project-shot"
          src="/images/work-chocacao.png"
          alt="The ChoCacao app: a sortable table of French communes ranked by temperature, with a colour scale."
          width="2000"
          height="1125"
          loading="lazy"
        />
      </a>
      <h2><a href="https://chocacao.leonardtschora.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ChoCacao — build time against run time</a></h2>
      <p class="what">A web app that finds the hottest and coolest communes in metropolitan France for any hour in the week ahead.</p>
      <p class="meta">Built solo in June 2026.</p>

      <ul class="stats">
        <li class="stat"><span class="stat-value">3 days</span><span class="stat-label">solo build</span></li>
        <li class="stat"><span class="stat-value">2,036</span><span class="stat-label">grid points</span></li>
        <li class="stat"><span class="stat-value">12</span><span class="stat-label">decision records</span></li>
      </ul>

      <h3>The decision worth naming</h3>
      <p>
        The expensive work happens once, offline. At build time a 25 km grid over France
        (2,036 points) is reverse-geocoded through <code>geo.api.gouv.fr</code> into about
        1,160 sampled communes. At run time the app loads that prebuilt index, fetches
        hourly temperatures from Open-Meteo in batches and caches daily, so the request path
        stays cheap. Twelve Architecture Decision Records, numbered 0001 to 0012.
      </p>

      <p class="card-links">
        <a class="button" href="https://chocacao.leonardtschora.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Live demo</a>
        <a class="button secondary" href="https://github.com/TschorAI/ChoCacao" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source</a>
      </p>
    </article>

    <article class="project" id="gengiscan">
      <a class="shot-link" href="https://gengiscan.leonardtschora.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true">
        <img
          class="project-shot"
          src="/images/work-gengiscan.png"
          alt="The gengiscan web app: an upload form for photos, scans or PDFs, beside a map of the Mongol Empire."
          width="1381"
          height="777"
          loading="lazy"
        />
      </a>
      <h2><a href="https://gengiscan.leonardtschora.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">gengiscan — a vision-LLM pipeline running in production</a></h2>
      <p class="what">A web app that turns photos, scans or PDFs of handwritten notes into a clean PDF, a Word file and Markdown, LaTeX equations and sketches included. It also runs as a Discord bot that commits each note to a GitHub repository.</p>
      <p class="meta">Bot built in April 2026, public web app since October 2026. 11 modules, deployed on Railway. The repository is private.</p>

      <ul class="stats">
        <li class="stat"><span class="stat-value">2 days</span><span class="stat-label">solo build</span></li>
        <li class="stat"><span class="stat-value">1,874</span><span class="stat-label">lines of Python</span></li>
        <li class="stat"><span class="stat-value">$0.03</span><span class="stat-label">per page, all-in</span></li>
      </ul>

      <h3>The decisions worth naming</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>
          A/B testing showed that the full OpenCV tonal chain — shadow removal, denoise,
          CLAHE, sharpen — amplifies paper texture into speckle and <em>hurts</em>{' '}
          vision-LLM accuracy. The default preprocessing is therefore deliberately minimal:
          perspective-correct and deskew only. A measured decision, not a stack preference.
        </li>
        <li>
          Messaging apps strip EXIF, so phone photos arrive sideways, and rotated
          handwriting makes a vision model hallucinate wholesale. A 480-pixel thumbnail is
          sent in all four rotations and the model picks the upright one, at about $0.003
          per page.
        </li>
        <li>
          Each page is routed by a cheap classifier: Haiku labels it, simple print goes to
          Sonnet, complex print and handwriting go to Opus. About $0.03 per page, under daily
          and monthly spend caps.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p class="card-links">
        <a class="button" href="https://gengiscan.leonardtschora.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Live demo</a>
      </p>
    </article>

    <article class="project" id="yeswekanji">
      <a class="shot-link" href="https://yeswekanji.leonardtschora.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true">
        <img
          class="project-shot"
          src="/images/work-yeswekanji.png"
          alt="The Yes we kanji app: a grid of Japanese kanji cards under a theme selector."
          width="2000"
          height="1124"
          loading="lazy"
        />
      </a>
      <h2><a href="https://yeswekanji.leonardtschora.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Yes we kanji — a one-day prototype, now on Google Cloud</a></h2>
      <p class="what">A web app for memorising Japanese kanji. Type any theme and get ten matching cards, with audio.</p>
      <p class="meta">Prototype: nine commits, all on 23 September 2026. Deployment and cross-device testing were mine, on Ubuntu, Android and iPhone browsers. Then ported to Google Cloud.</p>
      <p class="meta">Stack: BigQuery (data, vectors, review log) · Vertex AI Gemini with RAG through BigQuery <code>VECTOR_SEARCH</code> · Cloud Run · Cloud Storage · Text-to-Speech.</p>

      <ul class="stats">
        <li class="stat"><span class="stat-value">1 day</span><span class="stat-label">kick-off to live</span></li>
        <li class="stat"><span class="stat-value">1,987</span><span class="stat-label">jōyō kanji</span></li>
        <li class="stat"><span class="stat-value">30/30</span><span class="stat-label">test runs passed</span></li>
      </ul>

      <h3>The decisions worth naming</h3>
      <p>
        The prototype made no network calls at runtime: the kanji database was generated at
        build time from KANJIDIC2, and the 100 audio files were pre-generated MP3s. That is
        why it ran on free-tier hosting.
      </p>
      <p>
        The Google Cloud version adds themes generated on the fly. Gemini first checks the
        request is a theme and expands it; BigQuery <code>VECTOR_SEARCH</code> retrieves 30
        candidate kanji; Gemini picks ten; code rejects anything outside the candidates. On a
        10-theme test set run three times, that check step took the pass rate from 21/30 to
        30/30. Audio for new cards is synthesised once and kept in Cloud Storage. Eleven
        Architecture Decision Records, each recording what was rejected: six for the
        prototype, five for the Google Cloud version.
      </p>

      <p class="card-links">
        <a class="button" href="https://yeswekanji.leonardtschora.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Live demo</a>
        <a class="button secondary" href="https://github.com/TschorAI/yeswekanji-gcp" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source</a>
        <a class="button secondary" href="https://github.com/TschorAI/yeswekanji" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Prototype source</a>
      </p>
    </article>

    <h2>The method is the transferable asset</h2>
    <p>
      Twelve decision records in ChoCacao, eleven in Yes we kanji (six for the prototype,
      five for the Google Cloud version): two independent projects, two months apart, both writing down the alternatives they rejected. One repository
      with ADRs is a habit; two is a method. When AI agents write most of the code, the
      decision record is what survives the session — and what a client still owns after I
      leave.
    </p>

    <h2>Beyond my own products</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>A production platform, built from scratch.</strong> For the venture I
      co-founded I stood up the whole thing: OVH compute, a Railway-hosted database,
      Prefect orchestration, Argo CD and GitOps continuous delivery, GCP, VPN, multi-tenant
      Streamlit dashboards. I led the technical build, coordinated a team of five, and built
      an in-house team of AI coding agents that turns one command into a deployed
      forecasting pipeline for a new market.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Applied research, for a US client (name withheld).</strong> Consistency models
      and neural operators for diffusion-based image generation, aimed at cutting sampling
      steps and training cost. Research, not a shipped system: it is here for the depth, not
      the delivery.
    </p>

    <p class="cta">
      <a class="button" href={SITE.booking} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Book 30 minutes</a>
    </p>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  /* Project card. Page-specific: only /work/ has projects. */
  .project {
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    background: var(--surface);
    box-shadow: var(--shadow);
    padding: 1.25rem 1.35rem;
    margin: 1.5rem 0;
    /* Clears the sticky header when linked from /#app-cards. */
    scroll-margin-top: 5rem;
  }
  .project h2 {
    margin: 0 0 0.4rem;
    font-size: clamp(1.2rem, 2.5vw, 1.4rem);
  }
  .project h2 a { color: inherit; text-decoration: none; }
  .project h2 a:hover, .project h2 a:focus-visible { color: var(--accent); text-decoration: underline; }
  .project .what { margin: 0; }
  .project .meta {
    margin: 0.35rem 0 0;
    font-size: 0.88rem;
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }
  .project h3 {
    margin: 0 0 0.4rem;
    font-size: 0.78rem;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }
  .project p:last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }
  .project ul:not(.stats) { margin: 0; padding-left: 1.1rem; }
  .project ul:not(.stats) li { margin: 0.35rem 0; }

  /* The screenshot links to the live app; keyboard users get the same link on the title. */
  .shot-link { display: block; }
  .shot-link:hover .project-shot { border-color: var(--accent); }

  /* Reserved slot for a screenshot, first child of the card. */
  .project-shot {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    aspect-ratio: 16 / 9;
    object-fit: cover;
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: calc(var(--radius) - 4px);
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
  }

  .card-links {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    margin: 1.1rem 0 0;
  }
  .card-links .button { font-size: 0.93rem; padding: 0.45rem 0.95rem; }

  .cta { margin-top: 2rem; }

  @media (max-width: 26rem) {
    .project { padding: 1rem; }
    .card-links .button { flex: 1 1 auto; text-align: center; }
  }
</style>
